        <body>
            <div xml:id="d795">

                <pb facs="0785" n="773" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg773"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.00 Statement July 16, 1937/259</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Poland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Biddle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Warsaw</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-10">August 10, 1937</date>.<lb/>[Received August
                        26.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I have the honor to
                    attach hereto Colonel Beck’s letter and attached <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> addressed to you personally bearing on your statement of
                    July 16th, 1937.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <pb facs="0786" n="774" xml:id="pg774"/>
                <p>It might interest you to know that Colonel Beck remarked to me that he had never
                    before put so much effort in preparation of a declaration of this nature. In
                    fact, in my opinion, Poland has rarely made a commitment on policy of such
                    breadth. I take occasion to draw your particular attention to the adoption by
                    Minister Beck of the “good neighbor” policy in Eastern Europe. In my informal
                    discussions with him on political matters, he has frequently made use of the
                    terms <hi rendition="#italic">voisinage</hi> and “good neighbor” in describing a
                    new tendency in his policy. Undoubtedly, he has taken a leaf from the
                    President’s and your book in introducing the good neighbor policy into this none
                    too peaceful part of Europe.</p>
                <p>In closing may I not add my own congratulations upon the fundamental soundness of
                    your declaration? Indeed, it combines the highest ideals with the realistic and
                    I look for it to have a far-reaching constructive effect.</p>
                <p>With [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">A. J. Drexel Biddle,
                    Jr.</hi></signed></closer>



